# Call-site survey on the merged change (commit 3191e91)

Relevant to criterion 12 (the dead-utility decision), and to whether any
persisted data was written through DateFormatter's lenient parse.

## Performance mixins - callers outside lib/core/performance/
$ grep -rnE "PerformanceRepositoryMixin|PerformanceUseCaseMixin|measureDataSave|measureRepositoryOperation|measureUseCaseOperation" lib/ | grep -v lib/core/performance/
(no matches - zero call sites)

## DateFormatter - callers outside its own file
$ grep -rn DateFormatter lib/ integration_test/ | grep -v lib/core/utils/date_formatter.dart
(no matches - zero call sites, so nothing was ever persisted through it)

## How the tasks sample actually persists timestamps
$ grep -nE "created_at|updated_at" lib/features/tasks/data/models/task_model.dart
23:      createdAt: DateTime.parse(json['created_at'] as String),
24:      updatedAt: DateTime.parse(json['updated_at'] as String),
47:      'created_at': createdAt.toIso8601String(),
48:      'updated_at': updatedAt.toIso8601String(),

It calls DateTime.toIso8601String() directly, not DateFormatter, so the
strict-parse change cannot shift or reject any stored timestamp. No
storage migration is implied by this change.

That call does emit an offset-less string for a local DateTime - the same
class of defect as the JsonLogFormatter one - but it lives in
epic:feature-tasks and is filed as koniz-dev/flutter-starter#146 rather than
fixed here.

